## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests mastery of DAG dynamic programming, vertex-disjoint path packing, and careful state design to enforce disjointness while meeting exact cardinality constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological ordering enables DP by ensuring all dependencies are processed first
- Vertex-disjoint paths are guaranteed by tracking endpoint-specific states
- Path reconstruction requires storing predecessor pointers during computation
- Complexity: O(Mk + Nk) time, O(Nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode
const topo = topologicalSort(dag);
const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
const pred = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

for (const v of topo) {
  dp[v][0] = 0;
  const bestPrev = Math.max(...dp[v]);

  for (const u of dag.inEdges(v)) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
        // Extend existing path
        if (dp[u][t] + val[v] > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = dp[u][t] + val[v];
          pred[v][t] = u;
        }
        // Start new path
        if (t < k && bestPrev + val[v] > dp[v][t + 1]) {
          dp[v][t + 1] = bestPrev + val[v];
          pred[v][t + 1] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

const answer = Math.max(...dp.map(v => v[k]));
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // end a block at i
      for (let j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (pref[i+1] - pref[j]) * t;
        if (val > dp[i+1][t]) {
          dp[i+1][t] = val;
          par[i+1][t] = j;
        }
      }
      // skip i is implicit via dp[i+1][t] initialization from dp[i][t]
      if (dp[i][t] > dp[i+1][t]) {
        dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t];
        par[i+1][t] = -2; // indicate skip
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

